course: dictionary
lesson: 1291
title: Buy: one word, many uses
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Buy is a verb, and it has four forms: buy, buys, bought, buying. To buy something means you get it by paying money for it. Buy can also be a noun: a good buy is something worth the money you paid.
explain: Read this sentence: «Lily invited girls from both streets to her birthday, and her mum had to buy twice as many party bags.» Buy means to pay money to get the bags. Twice as many means two times the number, so the words around buy give you the clue.
explain: Look at «Saving means putting money aside, week after week, until you have enough for something bigger than one week's money can buy.» Here buy means get something by paying for it. Try swapping in "get by paying": it still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Buy means to get something by paying money for it.
fact: A buy is something bought, and a good buy is worth the money.
fact: Buy, buys, bought and buying are all forms of the same verb.
fact: In a shop maths problem, the buyer gets more things, so you add, and the buyer has less money, so you subtract the price.
fact: «Buy N packs of M» means N × M: 3 packs of 5 is 3 × 5 = 15.
exercise: «Lily's mum had to buy twice as many party bags.» What does buy mean here? | To sell something to a friend | To make something by hand | To throw something away | *To get something by paying money for it
exercise: Sam buys 4 packs of pencils with 6 pencils in each pack. How many pencils does he have altogether? | *24 | 46 | 10 | 4
exercise: Mia says, «That bike was a good buy.» What does a good buy mean? | A bag with no money in it | *Something bought that is worth the money | Something you were given for free that broke at once | Something you lost in the shop
test: What does «buy one, get one free» mean? | You get nothing at all | *You pay for one and get two | You pay for two and get one | You pay twice for one
test: A shop has 20 pounds. A toy costs 7 pounds. How much money is left after you buy it? | *13 | 7 | 27 | 14
exam: What is the past tense of buy? | buyed | buys | *bought | buying
